/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - WDM gives each signal a different wavelength λ.
   - C is the total bit rate in bit/s; N is the number of wavelength channels; B is the bit rate of one channel in bit/s.
   - Channel spacing can be given in frequency (GHz) or in wavelength (nm).
   - Δλ is the spacing in wavelength (m); λ is the operating wavelength (m); Δf is the spacing in frequency (Hz); c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s is the speed of light in vacuum.
   - At 1550 nm a 100 GHz step is 0.80 nm.
   - A multiplexer combines λ1…λN into one fiber; a demultiplexer separates them.
   - Four numbers describe a mux or demux.
   - The comb is the set of channel frequencies.
   - Capacity grows with N, but N is limited by the amplifier gain band (the erbium C-band is about 1530 to 1565 nm, see Optical amplifiers), laser wavelength drift, filter passband and crosstalk.
*/
