/* book: lead: A coupler splits light into two or more arms, an isolator passes light one way only, a circulator sends light round a fixed 1→2→3 route, and a fiber Bragg grating reflects one wavelength and passes the rest. None needs electrical power. They set how much power a WDM node loses and how much one channel leaks into its neighbour. */
/* book Figure 6.2.1: Top: the worked example’s coupler, with 250 µW in, 118 µW and 112 µW out and 4.0 nW back at port 2. Bottom: a grating of pitch 534.5 nm reflects λ2 = 1550 nm, so C1 drops λ2 at its port 3, the other channels pass to C2, and a new λ2 added at C2 port 1 is reflected by the grating and joins them at C2 */
