/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A coupler takes light in at one port and shares it among two or more output ports; run backwards, it combines several inputs into one output.
   - Name the ports of a 2×2 coupler this way: ports 1 and 2 on the input side, ports 3 and 4 on the output side.
   - Put all powers in the same unit (mW or µW); the ratio inside the log has no unit.
   - A laser diode is disturbed by light reflected back into it: the returning wave pulls its wavelength and adds noise (see Semiconductor laser diodes).
   - An isolator is a polarizer, a Faraday rotator and a second polarizer set at 45° to the first.
   - A circulator is the three-port version: light in at port 1 leaves by port 2, in at port 2 leaves by port 3, and in at port 3 leaves by port 1.
   - A fiber Bragg grating is a short piece of fiber whose core refractive index rises and falls periodically along the axis with pitch Λ.
   - λB is the reflected Bragg wavelength (m); neff is the effective index of the guided mode, about 1.45 in silica; Λ is the grating pitch (m).
   - All other wavelengths pass almost untouched, so in transmission the grating is a band-stop (notch) filter, with a stop band from a fraction of a nanometre to a few nanometres wide.
   - With two circulators the grating becomes an optical add-drop multiplexer.
*/
